    <h1>The Tallyman's<br>Codex of War</h1>
    <div class="sub">A Plain-Tongue Reckoning of the Rules of the 41st Millennium</div>
    <div class="rule-line"></div>
    <div class="by">
      Recovered from a battlefield manual of the Corpse-Emperor's servants, scoured of its lies,
      and re-inked in the bile of the <strong>RotFather's Vanguard</strong> so that even the
      slowest-witted plague-thrall might grasp the holy rhythm of slaughter.
      <br><br><em style="opacity:.7">Eleventh Edition Core Rules — explained, not merely transcribed.</em>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quote">Three things the Grandfather grants: patience, decay, and a clear set of rules</div>

  <!-- ===================== HOW TO READ ===================== -->
  <section id="how">
    <div class="kicker">Before the bloodletting</div>
    <h2>How to Read This Book</h2>
    <p>Warhammer 40,000 is a battle game played on a table with toy soldiers, dice, and a tape measure. Two armies face off, take turns, and try to complete a <em class="term">mission</em> — usually about grabbing patches of ground (objectives) and killing the other side. After a set number of rounds, whoever scored more wins.</p>
    <p>That is the whole thing. Everything below is just detail. To keep it painless, each rule comes with a few plain-speak boxes:</p>
    <div class="plain"><b>Plain speak</b>This is the rule in everyday words — what you actually do at the table.</div>
    <div class="scrawl"><b>Marginal scrawl — the Tallyman writes</b>An aside, a warning, or a Vanguard-flavoured way to remember the thing.</div>
    <p>A couple of symbols you'll see everywhere: a value of <span class="ic">"-"</span> in a stat means "none / not allowed," and <span class="ic">X+</span> (like 3+) means "roll this number <em>or higher</em> on a six-sided die to succeed."</p>
  </section>

